course: biology
lesson: 95
title: The nocturnal house: night animals by red light
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The nocturnal house swaps day and night: lights are dim in the zoo's daytime and bright at night when the zoo is closed, so the night animals are awake when you visit.
explain: The dim light is red, because many night animals cannot see red well, so you can watch them without them feeling watched.
explain: In the dark each animal has its own trick: the aye-aye taps on wood and listens, bats click and hear echoes, the kiwi sniffs with nostrils at the tip of its beak, the bushbaby gathers light with huge eyes.
explain: Shine a torch and eyes glow back at you, because many night animals have a mirror layer at the back of the eye; that is why the keeper asks you to switch it off.
explain: Dracula stories and Halloween cartoons made bats into scary bloodsuckers, but only three kinds of bat drink blood, and even they lap a few drops rather than suck.
fact: Nocturnal houses are dim during the day and lit at night, and many night animals cannot see red light well, so dim red light lets people see them.
fact: The slow loris is a small primate from Asia with huge eyes that moves slowly and quietly to sneak up on insects and stay unnoticed.
fact: The slow loris is one of very few venomous mammals; when a loris raises its arms while being tickled, it is frightened and getting ready to bite.
fact: Many lorises are caught illegally for the pet trade and have their teeth pulled out; all lorises are protected by international law.
fact: The aye-aye of Madagascar taps on wood, listens for hollow grub tunnels, gnaws a hole and hooks the grub out with the long thin middle one of the five fingers on each hand, doing a woodpecker's job.
fact: Bats are the only mammals that truly fly; many small bats find insects by echolocation, sending out clicks and listening for echoes, and bats are not blind.
fact: Only three of more than 1,400 kinds of bat drink blood; they live in Central and South America, make a tiny cut on sleeping animals and lap the blood.
fact: The kiwi of New Zealand is a flightless night bird, safe on the ground because old New Zealand had no ground-hunting mammals, with nostrils at the tip of its beak, and its egg is about a fifth of the mother's weight.
fact: Bushbabies are small African primates that leap several metres in the dark; their eyes are so big they cannot move them, so they turn their heads instead.
fact: Many night animals have a shiny layer at the back of the eye that bounces light back, so their eyes glow in a torch beam; bright light can hurt and stress them.
fact: Armadillos dig for insects at night with strong claws and are covered in bony plates; only the three-banded armadillos roll into a full ball.
fact: The nine-banded armadillo almost always has four identical babies.
fact: Night animals sleep by day in burrows, tree holes and dark corners, guided by their body clock; animals most active at dawn and dusk, like rabbits and deer, are called crepuscular.
exercise: Why is the nocturnal house dim during the zoo's daytime? | So the night animals are awake when visitors come | To save electricity, because the zoo pays huge bills | Because the animals are afraid of people | To keep the room warm
exercise: Why is the dim light in the nocturnal house red? | Many night animals cannot see red light well | Red light makes the animals feel calm and sleepy | Red light keeps away insects | It is the cheapest colour of bulb
exercise: Why does a slow loris move so slowly? | To sneak up on insects and stay unnoticed | Because it is always tired and half asleep | Because it is very old and its joints are stiff | Because its legs are too weak to move fast
exercise: What is special about the slow loris among mammals? | It is one of very few venomous mammals | It is the only mammal that lays eggs in a nest | It is the only mammal that can fly | It never sleeps
exercise: What does a slow loris lifting its arms while tickled really mean? | It is frightened and ready to defend itself | It is enjoying the tickle and wants some more | It wants a hug | It is asking for food
exercise: How does an aye-aye find grubs inside wood? | It taps on the wood and listens for hollow tunnels | It smells them through the bark | It sees the grubs glowing softly inside the dark wood | It waits for them to crawl out
exercise: What does an aye-aye use to hook a grub out? | Its long thin middle finger | Its long sticky tongue like a frog | Its sharp beak like a woodpecker | Its long bushy tail as a hook
exercise: Which are the only mammals that can truly fly? | Bats | Flying squirrels | Bushbabies | Sugar gliders
exercise: How do many small bats find insects in the dark? | They click and listen for the echoes | They smell them from far away | They see the warmth of insects with their eyes | They feel the air with their wings
exercise: How many kinds of bat drink blood? | Three | None at all | About 100 | All of them
exercise: Where are the kiwi's nostrils? | At the very tip of its beak | Just above its eyes, like a horse | On top of its head like a crest | Tucked away under its tiny wings
exercise: How big is a kiwi's egg compared with the mother? | About a fifth of her weight | About the size of a pea | Half as big as the mother | Bigger than the mother herself
exercise: How does a bushbaby look around with its huge eyes? | It turns its head, as its eyes cannot move | It rolls its huge eyes quickly from side to side | It closes one eye at a time | It looks only straight ahead
exercise: Why does the keeper ask the child to switch off his torch? | Bright light can hurt and stress night animals | The torch might scare other visitors | Torches are never allowed anywhere inside a zoo | The animals might eat the torch
exercise: Which armadillos can roll into a full ball? | Only the three-banded armadillos | All armadillos | Only the baby armadillos | None of them
exercise: How many babies does a nine-banded armadillo almost always have? | Four identical ones | One | Two | Ten
exercise: Do night animals ever sleep? | Yes, during the day in dark hiding places | No, never once in their whole lives | Only in winter | Only when the zoo closes
test: 3 armadillo mums each have their usual 4 babies. how many babies in total | 12 | 7 | 9 | 16
test: 5 bats hanging upside down, then 2 fly off. how many wings still hanging there | 6 | 10 | 4 | 14
test: Ella watched a video of a slow loris tickled with its arms up and said it was so cute. What is the truth? | It is frightened, and the trade harms wild lorises | It is laughing because it really likes being tickled | It is stretching after a nap | It is waving hello to the camera
test: In old Dracula stories and Halloween cartoons, a big bat sucks blood from people's necks. What would you fix? | Only three small kinds of bat lap a little blood, mostly from animals | All bats suck human blood at night | Vampire bats live only in the castles of Transylvania in Europe | Real vampire bats are as big as a person
test: "blind as a bat" my dad says. is he right | No, bats can see, and many also use echoes | Yes, all bats are fully blind from the day they are born | Yes, but only fruit bats | No, bats see only in daylight
test: A cat's eyes shine green in the car lights. Why? | A shiny layer at the back of the eye reflects the light | Its eyes make their own light, like a glow-worm does | Its eyes are full of fire | It is angry at the car
test: What would happen if the nocturnal house kept its lights bright all through the zoo day? | The night animals would sleep while visitors came | The animals would all turn into day animals overnight | The animals would glow in the bright light | Nothing, night animals are always awake
test: Woodpeckers do not live on Madagascar. Who does their job of finding grubs in wood there? | The aye-aye | The kiwi bird | The slow loris | The armadillo
test: Why could the kiwi live on the ground at night in New Zealand? | There were no ground mammals there to hunt it | It runs far too fast for any hunter to catch it | It is poisonous to every hunter | It could always fly away
test: Why do night animals so often have big eyes? | Big eyes let in as much dim light as possible | Big eyes make them look scary | Big eyes help them smell food much better in the dark | Big eyes keep them warm
test: A rabbit is busiest at dawn and dusk. What is that called? | Crepuscular | Nocturnal | Hibernating | Migratory
test: How many fingers does an aye-aye have on each hand, counting its long thin one? | Five | Three | Four | One
exam: Identify the principal reason for which nocturnal exhibits are illuminated with dim red light. | Numerous nocturnal species perceive red light poorly | Red light induces sleep in the animals | Red light repels insects from the enclosure and its visitors | Red light raises the temperature of the room
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the vampire bat? | It is one of three species that lap small quantities of blood | It sucks the blood of human beings throughout Europe by night | It is the largest of all bats | It is entirely blind and navigates by smell alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the slow loris raising its arms when touched. | The posture denotes fear and preparation for defence | The posture denotes enjoyment of the contact | The posture is a request for food | The posture denotes sleepiness
exam: It is given that two nine-banded armadillos produce litters of the customary size. Ascertain the aggregate number of offspring. | Eight | Four | Two | Eighteen
